<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/7th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Siege</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fall of Nineveh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/612-bce">612 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> · Also known as destroyed Nineveh · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medo-babylonian-conquest-of-the-assyrian-empire">Medo-Babylonian conquest of the Assyrian Empire</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a> was the destruction of the Assyrian capital in 612 BCE by a coalition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a>. It was the decisive blow to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a> and a founding moment of Median power. The event also appears in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> profile as part of the long history of the region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fall-of-nineveh.jpg" alt="Fall of Nineveh" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: (Mu-tamajo) むーたんじょ · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fall_of_nineveh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-assyrian-empire">Neo-Assyrian Empire</a>, which emerged in the 10th century BCE, had by the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a> controlled or held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a> lands from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. After his death in 631 BCE bitter wars of succession weakened it, and from 625 BCE its hold on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> began to fade. By their own records the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> had been brutal rulers, and they had made many enemies. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Chaldeans</a>, with Babylonian help, took control of much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>, founding the Neo-Babylonian Empire under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabopolassar">Nabopolassar</a>, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a> under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyaxares">Cyaxares</a> rebuilt their army after an early defeat.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a> was a political capital, the home of a great library of Akkadian tablets and the recipient of tribute from across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a>. The surviving accounts were all written by its enemies: a Babylonian chronicle, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> tradition attributed to Nahum and Egyptian records.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Fall of Nineveh Chronicle describes a 12-year struggle. In 616 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> defeated the Assyrian army and sacked towns upriver; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> drove them back to Takritain but were beaten there. The Babylonians then allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a>, and in 614 BCE the Medes sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assur">Assur</a>. In 612 BCE the Babylonians joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyaxares">Cyaxares</a> before Nineveh, besieged it from May for three months and in August broke through, plundering and burning the city, with the Medes the decisive force. King Sin-shar-ishkun was killed. Diodorus records that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> flooded the city, and one theory holds that the attackers diverted the Khosr River to breach the walls.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Sin-shar-ishkun's brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashur-uballit-ii">Ashur-uballit II</a> fought his way out and set up a last capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> and their allies campaigned against him for several more years, once against an Egyptian-Assyrian army, and his fate is unknown: he may have died at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-harran">Fall of Harran</a> in 609 BCE or at Carchemish in 605 BCE. Babylon became the imperial centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> for the first time in over a thousand years. Archaeology shows <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a> was largely depopulated in the following centuries, and the burning of its palace may have helped preserve its clay texts. A garbled memory of the fall gave rise to the legend of King Sardanapalus.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fall of Nineveh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Medes sacked Assur in 614 BCE, the Babylonians and Medes together <b>destroyed Nineveh</b> in 612 BCE, and the last Assyrian king, Ashur-uballit II, abandoned his refuge at Harran in 610 BCE and, after the fall of Harran, failed with Egyptian help to retake it in 609.</blockquote><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
